This patch is a bit problematic with unix multiuser setuid/setgid
installations. The safe way to use this is to set a constant dump filename
in a directory only the setuid/setgid user/group has write access (like
playground or save game directory). Setting the dump filename to point to
any directory where ordinary users have write access is unacceptable because
of symlink attacks. A malicious player could then overwrite level, bones and
save game files (or anything else the setuid/gid has write access to).

Dumping to a constant location even with %n in the name is (probably)
vulnerable to a sort of "denial of service" attack. A malicious player can
start a player with the same name as a hapless player using the dump option,
and then just as hapless player gets his dump file written, the malicious
player overwrites the file with his own. Dunno if this _really_ is a
problem, though...

Also, the %n substitution may be problematic with player names like "../.."
if the constant dump filename is like /path/%n.something (it should be
/path/something.%n). One correction would be to change the substitution to
uid instead of name, a bit like the save files are named.

